.TH "zelta\-prune" "8" "" "" "System Manager\(cqs Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zelta prune\f[R] \- report snapshot prune candidates
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]zelta prune\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zelta prune\f[R] reports snapshots on an endpoint dataset tree that
are candidates for pruning.
It does not destroy snapshots.
Use \f[B]zprune(8)\f[R] with the same options after reviewing a
strategy.
.PP
Only \f[I]endpoint\f[R] is evaluated for prune candidates.
An optional match endpoint is never destroyed; it is used only for match
validation and \f[B]\(enprune\-guard\f[R] protection.
.PP
A pruning strategy combines scope filters, snapshot protections, and
retention options.
Scope filters first narrow the dataset tree or snapshot names.
Clone and replication checks protect snapshots within that scope.
Retention options protect snapshots by count, age, or retention grid.
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-size\f[R] can further limit candidates to an estimated
reclaim target.
.PP
All selected rules stack.
A snapshot is a prune candidate only if it remains in scope and no
protection or retention rule keeps it.
For example, the default strategy is:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard=latest\f[R]: Use the latest common snapshot
between \f[I]endpoint\f[R] and the match endpoint, when one exists, as a
protected boundary.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-num=30\f[R]: Keep the newest 30 snapshots older than
that protected boundary.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-time=30days\f[R]: Keep snapshots from the last 30 days.
.PP
The count and time windows overlap.
Older than a protected replication boundary, the default keeps the
newest 30 snapshots or all snapshots from the last 30 days when that
protects more.
The boundary and everything newer remain protected separately.
Specifying any retention option replaces the count and time defaults.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Endpoint
.TP
\f[I]endpoint\f[R]
Dataset tree containing snapshots to evaluate.
\f[B]zprune\f[R] destroys candidates on this endpoint only.
.SS Match Endpoint
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-match\-endpoint\f[R] \f[I]guard\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-guard\-endpoint\f[R] \f[I]guard\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard\-endpoint\f[R] \f[I]guard\f[R]
Second dataset tree used only for match validation and
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard\f[R] protection (and as the grid/latest boundary
peer).
Snapshots on \f[I]guard\f[R] are never destroyed.
.RS
A second positional operand is accepted as the same match endpoint
(\f[CR]zelta prune\f[R] \f[I]endpoint\f[R] \f[I]guard\f[R]) but is not
shown in the synopsis; prefer the flag.
Do not combine \f[CR]\-\-match\-endpoint\f[R] with a second positional
operand.
.RE
.PP
Without a match endpoint, \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] applies no prune guard.
\f[B]zprune\f[R] requires either \f[CR]\-\-match\-endpoint\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-\-no\-prune\-guard\f[R].
.SS Output Options
General output, logging, and help options work as described in
\f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R].
The following options are especially relevant to pruning.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-dryrun\f[R], \f[CR]\-n\f[R]
Display the \f[B]zfs list\f[R] command(s) that would be used.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-no\-ranges\f[R]
Disable range compression.
By default, consecutive snapshots are emitted as ZFS snapshot ranges
suitable for \f[B]zfs destroy(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R], \f[CR]\-v\f[R]
Increase verbosity.
Specify once for operational detail, twice for debug output.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-visual\f[R]
Replace the candidate list with \f[CR]🔹\f[R] for each protected
snapshot and \f[CR]❌\f[R] for each prune candidate.
Snapshots appear in creation order, oldest first.
Terminals may wrap long rows.
Visual output is written directly to standard output rather than through
the normal logging modes.
.SS Filters
Scope filters are processed before retention options.
For example, \f[CR]\-\-depth=1\f[R] considers only the top\-level
dataset and ignores its children.
Snapshots excluded by a scope filter cannot become prune candidates.
Snapshots that are clone origins are always protected.
.PP
The following global filters work as they do for other Zelta verbs; see
\f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-depth\f[R], \f[CR]\-d\f[R] \f[I]LEVELS\f[R]
Limit dataset\-tree recursion depth.
A depth of \f[CR]1\f[R] includes only the specified dataset.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R], \f[CR]\-X\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Exclude datasets or snapshots matching \f[I]PATTERN\f[R].
See \f[I]INCLUDE AND EXCLUDE PATTERNS\f[R] in
\f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-include\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Include only datasets or snapshots matching \f[I]PATTERN\f[R].
See \f[I]INCLUDE AND EXCLUDE PATTERNS\f[R] in
\f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-name\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Match snapshot names with a glob (\f[CR]*\f[R], \f[CR]?\f[R]), without a
leading \f[CR]\(at\f[R].
Built\-ins are \f[CR]zfsnap\f[R] (\f[CR]zfsnap_*\f[R]),
\f[CR]truenas\f[R] (\f[CR]auto\-*\f[R]), and \f[CR]sanoid\f[R]
(\f[CR]autosnap_*\f[R]).
.PP
Additionally, \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] protects candidates based on their
replication state when a match endpoint is supplied.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard=latest\f[R]
When \f[I]endpoint\f[R] and the match endpoint have a common snapshot,
protect the latest match and everything newer on \f[I]endpoint\f[R].
If no match exists, evaluate from the latest snapshot on
\f[I]endpoint\f[R].
This is the default when a match endpoint is given.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard=unsynced\f[R]
Require the candidate snapshot to be present on the match endpoint.
Snapshots not confirmed there are protected.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard=none\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-no\-prune\-guard\f[R]
Do not require a match endpoint or confirm that candidates exist on it.
If a match endpoint is supplied, its latest common snapshot still
defines the retention boundary.
\f[B]zprune\f[R] requires this option when a match endpoint is omitted.
.SS Retention Options
Retention options apply within the scope selected by
\f[CR]\-\-depth\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-exclude\f[R],
and \f[CR]\-\-prune\-guard\f[R].
Count, time, and grid options overlap: a snapshot is protected when any
selected retention option keeps it.
If no retention options are given, \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] uses
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-num=30 \-\-prune\-time=30days\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-grid\f[R] \f[I]GRID\f[R]
Keep snapshots in one or more aging intervals written as
[\f[I]COUNT\f[R]\f[CR]x\f[R]]\f[I]INTERVAL\f[R].
For example, \f[CR]30x1day, 52x1week, 1year\f[R] keeps one snapshot per
day for 30 days, then one per week for 52 weeks, then one per year for
all older history.
.PP
Grid terms are evaluated from left to right.
A term without \f[I]COUNT\f[R] applies to all remaining history.
Separate terms with commas or vertical bars.
Whitespace is allowed.
.PP
Grid ages are measured backward from the latest common snapshot between
\f[I]endpoint\f[R] and the match endpoint when one exists, or from the
latest snapshot or bookmark on \f[I]endpoint\f[R] otherwise.
\f[B]zelta prune\f[R] protects the newest snapshot found in each
interval.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-num\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Keep the newest \f[I]N\f[R] snapshots.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-size\f[R] \f[I]SIZE\f[R]
Select the oldest remaining snapshots until their estimated reclaim
reaches at least \f[I]SIZE\f[R].
Suffixes such as \f[CR]M\f[R], \f[CR]G\f[R], and \f[CR]T\f[R] are
supported.
.PP
Like all other options, each dataset is evaluated separately.
On a dataset tree, every dataset is evaluated against the full
\f[I]SIZE\f[R].
Use \f[CR]\-\-depth=1\f[R] or another filter to limit one dataset.
The estimate assumes sequential oldest\-first pruning; use the
\f[B]zprune(8)\f[R] preview for a more accurate estimate of reclaimed
space.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-prune\-time\f[R] \f[I]TIME\f[R]
Keep snapshots newer than \f[I]TIME\f[R] relative to the current time.
.SS Duration Syntax
Bare numbers are seconds.
Use unambiguous units for anything else:
.IP
.EX
seconds   s, sec, second, seconds
minutes   mi, min, minute, minutes
hours     h, hour, hours
days      d, day, days
weeks     w, week, weeks
months    mo, mon, month, months
years     y, year, years
.EE
.PP
The unit \f[CR]m\f[R] is ambiguous and is rejected.
Use \f[CR]mi\f[R] for minutes or \f[CR]mo\f[R] for months.
Months are treated as 30 days and years as 365.25 days.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
By default, \f[B]zelta prune\f[R] outputs ZFS snapshot names or ranges,
one per line:
.IP
.EX
pool/dataset\(atoldest_snapshot%newest_snapshot
.EE
.PP
With \f[CR]\-\-no\-ranges\f[R], each candidate snapshot is emitted
individually:
.IP
.EX
pool/dataset\(atsnapshot
.EE
.PP
The output is suitable for review and for \f[B]zprune(8)\f[R].
Manual piping to \f[B]zfs destroy\f[R] is discouraged; \f[B]zprune\f[R]
previews candidates with \f[B]zfs destroy \-nvp\f[R] and prompts before
deletion.
.PP
With \f[CR]\-\-visual\f[R], each dataset is followed by one symbol per
snapshot:
.IP
.EX
tank/data
❌❌❌❌🔹🔹🔹🔹
.EE
.PP
Snapshots run from oldest to newest.
A \f[CR]🔹\f[R] is protected; a \f[CR]❌\f[R] is a prune candidate.
The symbols show the result of the complete strategy, including filters,
prune guards, and overlapping retention options.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect the default strategy before working with its candidate list:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
When a common snapshot exists, the default protects that latest match
and everything newer.
Among older snapshots, it also protects the newest 30 and all snapshots
from the last 30 days.
Diamonds may extend farther into history when the time window protects
more than the count window.
.PP
Select the oldest snapshots until their estimated reclaim reaches 150
GiB.
Limiting depth makes the reclaim apply only to \f[CR]tank/data\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-prune\-size=150G \-\-depth=1 tank/data
.EE
.PP
Add a match endpoint when old snapshots should be candidates only after
reaching the peer:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-prune\-size=150G \-\-prune\-guard=unsynced \(rs
    \-\-match\-endpoint=backup:tank/data tank/data
.EE
.PP
Comparing this with the preceding size\-only view reveals old history
that the replica guard protects because it is missing from the match
endpoint.
.PP
Compare count and time windows independently:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-prune\-num=30 tank/data
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-prune\-time=30days tank/data
.EE
.PP
Limit the strategy to snapshots whose names begin with \f[CR]zelta\f[R].
Snapshots with other names remain protected:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-include=\(aq\(atzelta*\(aq tank/data
.EE
.PP
Keep one snapshot per week throughout all history considered by the
filters:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-prune\-grid=1week tank/data
.EE
.PP
Apply a longer data\-lifecycle grid:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-visual \-\-prune\-grid=\(aq30x1day, 52x1week, 1year\(aq \(rs
    tank/data
.EE
.PP
After inspecting a strategy, report its candidates in the default
pipeable format:
.IP
.EX
zelta prune \-\-prune\-grid=\(aq30x1day, 52x1week, 1year\(aq tank/data
.EE
.PP
Preview the exact \f[B]zfs destroy\f[R] commands and confirm destructive
pruning.
Because this example has no match endpoint, \f[B]zprune\f[R] requires an
explicit \f[CR]\-\-no\-prune\-guard\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
zprune \-\-no\-prune\-guard \(rs
    \-\-prune\-grid=\(aq30x1day, 52x1week, 1year\(aq tank/data
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success and non\-zero on error.
.SH NOTES
\f[B]zelta prune\f[R] is nondestructive: it only plans candidates.
Use \f[B]zprune(8)\f[R] when you intend to destroy snapshots, and review
the preview carefully.
.PP
This command is driven by the same comparison engine as \f[B]zelta
match\f[R].
See \f[B]zelta\-match(8)\f[R] for endpoint matching behavior.
